\f[CR]pkgs.<name>\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
the Haskell package set
.PP
It ignores version requests, since nixpkgs carries a single version.
\f[CR]cabal\f[R] is here because the tool\(cqs name is not the name of
the package carrying it.
An entry of the project\(cqs own replaces it.
.PP
\f[I]Type:\f[R] attribute set of package
.PP
\f[I]Default:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{ cabal = config.nixpkgs.pkgs.cabal\-install; }
.EE
.PP
\f[I]Example:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{ haskell\-language\-server = pkgs.haskell\-language\-server; }
.EE
.PP
\f[I]Declared by:\f[R] \- \c
.UR file://%3Cnix-haskell%3E/modules/nixpkgs
/modules/nixpkgs
.UE \c
.SS nixpkgs.options.use\-plan
Take the project\(cqs structure (local packages, their directories,
source\-repository\-packages) from the cabal plan of the haskell.nix
driver instead of the root of the source.
The plan is cabal\(cqs own reading of cabal.project, so globs,
optional\-packages and conditionals are exact.
The cost is evaluating the haskell.nix toolchain (import from
derivation).
The driver still builds the packages from nixpkgs.
.PP
This turns \f[CR]exact\-configuration\f[R] on by default.
A plan brings in packages with version bounds this driver has no solver
to satisfy, so reading a cabal.project needs the bound relief as well.
.PP
\f[I]Type:\f[R] boolean
.PP
\f[I]Default:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
false
.EE
.PP
