\f[I]Declared by:\f[R] \- \c
.UR file://%3Cnix-haskell%3E/modules/cross/wasm
/modules/cross/wasm
.UE \c
.SS wasm\-optimize
\f[B]A function, not a setting.\f[R] A project calls it and uses the
result.
Assign it only to replace what the call does.
.PP
A wasm binary optimized and stripped.
It takes the file, not the package that carries it.
It yields the file, not a directory holding it, so the caller installs
it under any name:
.IP
.EX
wasm\-optimize {
  platform = \(dqwasi32\(dq;
  package = \(dqfrontend\(dq;
  exe = \(dqfrontend\(dq;
  wasm = \(dq${frontend}/bin/frontend.wasm\(dq;
}
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]platform\f[R], \f[CR]package\f[R] and \f[CR]exe\f[R] are only
lookup keys for the settings.
Each can be left out, and an omitted key states nothing.
.PP
Each field of the \f[CR]wasm\-opt\f[R] settings resolves on its own.
The most specific layer that states a field decides it, in this order:
.IP "1." 3
\f[CR]platforms.<platform>.packages.<package>.components.exes.<exe>.wasm\-opt\f[R]
.IP "2." 3
\f[CR]platforms.<platform>.packages.<package>.wasm\-opt\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
\f[CR]platforms.<platform>.wasm\-opt\f[R]
.IP "4." 3
\f[CR]packages.<package>.components.exes.<exe>.wasm\-opt\f[R], then
\f[CR]packages.<package>.wasm\-opt\f[R]
.IP "5." 3
\f[CR]wasm\-opt\f[R] at the top level, the only layer that holds every
field.
.PP
The settings come from the project\(cqs own values, not a driver\(cqs.
This function runs on a built artifact, outside any driver.
.PP
\f[I]Type:\f[R] function that evaluates to a(n) package
.PP
\f[I]Default:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
<nix\-haskell>/libs/bundle\-optimizer/wasm\-opt/run.nix, run with the settings the named target, package and executable resolve to
.EE
.PP
\f[I]Declared by:\f[R] \- \c
.UR file://%3Cnix-haskell%3E/modules/cross/wasm
/modules/cross/wasm
.UE \c
